// DESIRED SERVING HOSTS ARE A PROJECTION OF THE ROLE ASSIGNMENT, NOT A LIST AUTHORED HERE.
//
// This row named both Sparks. Nothing was wrong with it while both machines served; it became wrong
// the moment one of them was wanted as a trainer, and the shape is why: a hand-authored host list is
// a second answer to "what is this machine for", so retiring srv6 from serving meant editing a
// literal in this file while the intent lived nowhere. Deleting the name would have been a change
// with no reviewable subject.
//
// gunbc.spark.cell_role owns the fact. Serving desired state asks it which cells serve, so srv6
// leaves this roster because its ROLE changed -- one edit, in the authority, that the training-side
// roster reads from too. The two rosters cannot disagree about a host because they are one fold of
// one list read in two directions.
data spark_serving_desired_hosts: List<HostIdentity> = spark_serving_cell_hosts()

// OPERATOR DECLARATION, as amended (2026-08-12, model amended 2026-09-01 by SPARK-PAIR-0 P1): LAN
// boundary, ollama runtime, deepseek-v4-gguf:no-h3-stop model, executor-owned serving process under
// gunbc-automation. Runtime closure identity derives from extdeps.ollama.binary_release cited digest
// — materialization binds through gunbc.package_delivery (#8225), not a parallel install path. Model
// identity is the manifest digest from /api/tags, not the serving label.
//
// The model is named here in the same breath as the boundary and the runtime because all three are
// one declaration of current intent. Amending the row below without amending this sentence would
// leave two statements of what the cell is FOR, which is the meaning fork this file otherwise
// spends its length preventing.

data spark_serving_desired_bind_listen: SparkServingBindListen = SparkServingBindListen {
  host: "0.0.0.0" as NonEmptyStr,
  port: ollama_default_port,
}

fn spark_serving_desired_bind_host_port() -> String {
  spark_serving_bind_listen_wire(bind: spark_serving_desired_bind_listen)
}

// A LISTEN ADDRESS IS NOT A CONNECT ADDRESS, and the two were one string.
//
// The unit binds `0.0.0.0` because the endpoint is meant to be reachable across the LAN. Nothing
// CONNECTS to `0.0.0.0`: it names "every local interface" to a listener, and a probe that dialled it
// would be relying on a kernel convenience that says nothing about whether the intended interface is
// serving. The on-host readiness probe therefore dials loopback on the same port -- same service,
// stated as the address a client actually uses -- and the port is taken from the one bind row so the
// two cannot drift into naming different services.
data spark_serving_local_probe_host: NonEmptyStr = "127.0.0.1" as NonEmptyStr

fn spark_serving_local_probe_host_port() -> String {
  join([spark_serving_local_probe_host as String, ":", to_string(spark_serving_desired_bind_listen.port)], "")
}

// The path comes from `extdeps.ollama.api` `ollama_endpoint_path`, not from a literal here. Writing
// "/api/version" would be a second spelling of a fact the cited endpoint model already owns, and the
// two would drift silently the first time upstream moved it.
fn spark_serving_local_endpoint_url(endpoint: OllamaEndpoint) -> String {
  join(["http://", spark_serving_local_probe_host_port(), ollama_endpoint_path(endpoint: endpoint)], "")
}

fn spark_serving_local_api_version_url() -> String {
  spark_serving_local_endpoint_url(endpoint: ApiVersion)
}

data spark_serving_desired_endpoint_intent: ServingEndpointIntent = UnauthenticatedEndpoint

// THE ONE ROW THAT SELECTS. Everything downstream -- the reference, the artifact identity, the
// manifest digest a materializer checks bytes against, and the blob closure it installs -- is
// DERIVED from this manifest rather than named again beside it.
//
// It was not always so, and the failure that forced this shape is worth keeping. When the reference
// alone moved to 120b, the materializer went on naming the 20b digest and the 20b closure as
// free-standing constants: the plan then fetched the 120b manifest by reference, declared the 20b
// digest as its expected checksum, and installed the 20b blobs. The checksum step would have caught
// it -- loudly, which is the system working -- but only at apply time on a real host, because
// nothing in the corpus tied the three facts together. Three independent spellings of "which model"
// is DESIGN §3's fork sitting in the input to an install plan.
data spark_serving_desired_manifest: OllamaResolvedManifest = deepseek_v4_no_h3_stop_manifest

data spark_serving_desired_model_ref: OllamaModelRef = ollama_resolved_manifest_reference(m: spark_serving_desired_manifest)

// DESIRED MODEL IDENTITY IS A RESOLUTION, NOT A LITERAL.
//
// What this row used to be: a Sha256Digest typed in here, wrapped in an OllamaModelDigest, and handed
// to model_artifact_from_observed_digest -- a door whose authority says the digest came off an
// endpoint. Nothing had observed it. The literal was 49124fc2c62223..., correct when authored on
// 2026-08-12 and wrong from the moment upstream republished the tag.
//
// WHAT THE REPUBLISH DID, since the failure was read as host drift for a week. On 2026-08-19 the
// registry, srv6's manifest file, and srv6's /api/tags all answered 17052f91a42e97... The pin
// disagreed with all three, so the model member reconciled as drift on every pass and planned a pull
// forever -- and the pull could not have fixed it, because MaterializeModelArtifact runs
// `ollama pull gpt-oss:20b` and fetches whatever the tag names NOW. A digest pin behind a
// tag-addressed remedy is unenforceable by construction.
//
// A RE-SELECTION IS AN OPERATOR DECISION, AND THE 2026-08-19 ONE ESTABLISHED THE PATTERN: desired
// state names the identity the reference currently resolves to. That is not "follow latest at apply"
// -- execution still names one exact identity and never consults upstream to find it. It is an
// authored change of desired state, and where the result already exists on the host, reconciliation
// plans zero model action rather than a pull.
//
// THE SELECTION THIS ROW CARRIES NOW (operator decision, 2026-09-02): deepseek-v4-gguf:no-h3-stop.
// gpt-oss:120b is RETIRED -- "gpt-oss:120b will not be used anymore" -- and retiring it is a safety
// fix rather than a preference. Both serving cells were already running the DeepSeek build while
// this row still named gpt-oss, so desired state and served state disagreed about the one fact the
// converge slice actuates on. A wet converge in that state would not have drifted toward the
// selection; it would have ASSERTED it, replacing a working service with a model nobody wanted.
// Desired state naming something other than what is served is not a stale row, it is a loaded gun.
//
// WHAT IS DIFFERENT IN KIND ABOUT THIS SELECTION, and it reaches further than the name. Every
// previous row here named an upstream library tag. This one names an artifact that was CREATED ON
// THE CELLS by POST /api/create -- the repair of a parent whose `PARAMETER stop "###"` silently
// truncated every answer that reached a markdown H3. It has no registry, so three things that were
// previously implicit had to become modeled facts: the manifest's provenance
// (gunbc.ollama_model_resolution OllamaManifestProvenance), the refusal of a registry-fetch install
// plan for it (gunbc.spark.serving_converge_realize), and the runtime-support fact that the ollama
// catalogue cannot answer for a model ollama does not publish
// (gunbc.spark.serving_model_admission).
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE FOR CONVERGENCE, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS A REAL LIMITATION AND NOT A
// FOOTNOTE: this cell can be converged onto its serving artifact but CANNOT REPRODUCE IT. The
// materializer refuses rather than emitting a fetch that cannot succeed, so a cell that lost its
// model store could not be rebuilt from this desired state. That is missing work, it is named here
// so it stays ranked, and the refusal is what keeps it visible instead of failing at apply time.
//
// The capacity claim is re-derived by spark_serving_observe_ci_wet, which reads the cell's /api/tags
// and its host memory; the resolved identity and its four readings are
// deepseek_v4_no_h3_stop_resolution. Neither the model's footprint nor the host's memory is
// transcribed here, because a number copied into prose is unreachable from the instrument that owns
// it and rots without either end being touched.
//
// WHAT THIS ROW STILL DOES NOT DECIDE. How many sessions the cell serves concurrently is not modeled
// anywhere in desired state, so it is inherited from whatever the launcher defaults to. That default
// is not observed by any instrument in this corpus either -- which is the whole defect, and the
// reason no value for it is quoted here: there is nothing to cite. Selecting the model does not
// address it, and the two facts trade against each other inside one memory budget, because the slot
// count divides the context window. The concurrency row is P1b and is deliberately not smuggled in
// here, because its value is an operator decision this row cannot make.
//
// The evidence, the superseded 2026-08-12 identity, and the resolution timestamp live in
// gunbc.ollama_model_resolution, whose carrier cannot be constructed without its evidence. This
// module consumes that resolution; it no longer manufactures an identity of its own.

type SparkServingDesiredAdmission
  = SparkServingDesiredAdmitted { serving_release: SparkServingRelease }
  | SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_serving_desired_model_artifact() -> ModelArtifact {
  model_artifact_from_resolution(
    publisher: "ollama" as NonEmptyStr,
    resolution: spark_serving_desired_manifest.resolution,
  )
}

// THE MODEL AXIS IS DECIDED BEFORE THE PLATFORM AXIS, because for a locally materialized artifact
// the platform verdict cannot answer at all -- the catalogue returns Uncatalogued and the cause it
// reports would name the model as unsupported when what is actually true is that this repository,
// not ollama, owns the support fact. Routing the local arm around the catalogue is not skipping a
// check: gunbc.spark.serving_model_admission applies a STRICTER one, requiring an observed load at
// the exact required release rather than a version floor.
fn spark_serving_desired_release_admission() -> SparkServingDesiredAdmission {
  match spark_serving_model_axis(manifest: spark_serving_desired_manifest) {
    SparkServingModelAxisRefused { cause: c } =>
      SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c }
    SparkServingModelAxisLocalAttested { runtime_version: _ } =>
      SparkServingDesiredAdmitted {
        serving_release: SparkServingRelease {
          runtime: RuntimeArtifactIdentity {
            closure_digest: ollama_runtime_materialization_identity_for_required_release().digest as NonEmptyStr,
          },
          model: spark_serving_desired_model_artifact(),
        },
      }
    SparkServingModelAxisUpstreamCatalogued =>
      spark_serving_desired_upstream_release_admission()
  }
}

fn spark_serving_desired_upstream_release_admission() -> SparkServingDesiredAdmission {
  match ollama_runtime_model_compatibility_verdict(
    binary_release: ollama_required_release,
    platform: OllamaNvidiaGb10Spark,
    architecture: OllamaLinuxArm64,
    model: spark_serving_desired_model_ref,
  ) {
    OllamaPlatformSupportApplicable =>
      SparkServingDesiredAdmitted {
        serving_release: SparkServingRelease {
          runtime: RuntimeArtifactIdentity {
            closure_digest: ollama_runtime_materialization_identity_for_required_release().digest as NonEmptyStr,
          },
          model: spark_serving_desired_model_artifact(),
        },
      }
    OllamaPlatformSupportUnmet { cause: c } =>
      SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c }
    OllamaPlatformSupportUnobserved { cause: c } =>
      SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c }
    OllamaPlatformSupportUncatalogued { cause: c } =>
      SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c }
  }
}

data spark_serving_desired_admission: SparkServingDesiredAdmission = spark_serving_desired_release_admission()

type SparkServingFleetDesired
  = SparkServingFleetDesiredReady { occurrences: List<SparkServingOccurrence> }
  | SparkServingFleetDesiredRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_serving_occurrence(host: HostIdentity, serving_release: SparkServingRelease, profile: ServingLaunchProfile) -> SparkServingOccurrence {
  SparkServingOccurrence {
    host: host,
    release_identity: spark_serving_release_identity(spec: serving_release),
    launch_profile: profile,
  }
}

fn spark_serving_fleet_desired_from_admission() -> SparkServingFleetDesired {
  match spark_serving_desired_admission {
    SparkServingDesiredAdmitted { serving_release: r } =>
      match spark_serving_launch_admission(serving_release: r) {
        LaunchRefused { cause: c } => SparkServingFleetDesiredRefused { cause: c }
        LaunchAdmitted { profile: p } =>
          SparkServingFleetDesiredReady {
            occurrences: map(
              spark_serving_desired_hosts,
              h => spark_serving_occurrence(host: h, serving_release: r, profile: p),
            ),
          }
      }
    SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c } =>
      SparkServingFleetDesiredRefused { cause: c }
  }
}

data spark_serving_fleet_desired: SparkServingFleetDesired = spark_serving_fleet_desired_from_admission()

// THE LAUNCH PROFILE IS ADMITTED OR REFUSED; IT IS NEVER DEFAULTED. The contract's window and slot
// count come from the observation recorded for THIS release under THIS engine, and from nowhere
// else. A refusal carries the key, so the operator's next action is named.
type ServingLaunchAdmission
  = LaunchAdmitted { profile: ServingLaunchProfile }
  | LaunchRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_serving_launch_admission(serving_release: SparkServingRelease) -> ServingLaunchAdmission {
  let key = ServingRealizationKey {
    release_identity: spark_serving_release_identity(spec: serving_release),
    engine: spark_serving_desired_engine,
  }
  match serving_config_admission(key: key) {
    ConfigRefusedUnobserved { key: k } => LaunchRefused { cause: serving_config_refusal_text(key: k) }
    ConfigAdmitted { config: c, observation: _ } =>
      LaunchAdmitted { profile: ServingLaunchProfile {
        engine: spark_serving_desired_engine,
        contract: ServingContract {
          bind: spark_serving_desired_bind_listen,
          default_context: c.default_context,
          serving_slots: c.serving_slots,
        },
      } }
  }
}

// The fleet's admitted profile, for consumers that need the contract and can carry a refusal.
fn spark_serving_desired_launch_admission() -> ServingLaunchAdmission {
  match spark_serving_desired_admission {
    SparkServingDesiredRefused { cause: c } => LaunchRefused { cause: c }
    SparkServingDesiredAdmitted { serving_release: r } => spark_serving_launch_admission(serving_release: r)
  }
}

// THE ENGINE THIS FLEET ACTUALLY RUNS, authored for the first time. Until now the deployment in
// service could not be declared at all -- the launch profile named Ollama in its type, so the
// vLLM tensor-parallel pair that has been serving this fleet had no representation, which is
// exactly why it lived in hand-written units and why convergence kept restoring the engine we had
// abandoned. A model that cannot describe what is running cannot converge toward it.
//
// WORLD SIZE 2, not 4: the pairs sit on point-to-point direct-attach rails and the only path
// between pairs is the WiFi LAN, so a group of four cannot route its per-layer all-reduce at all.
// That topology is already modeled in gunbc.spark.fabric_link_desired rather than restated here.
data spark_serving_desired_engine: ServingEngine = VllmTensorParallel {
  world_size: 2,
}

// The unit's window is DERIVED from the serving policy rather than authored beside it. This is the
// causal link the repair adds: the policy is the single authority, the unit text is a function of
// it, and the unit's content identity is a function of the text -- so a policy change cannot fail
// to reach the file, and cannot reach it without producing a member delta.
fn spark_serving_terminal_intent() -> SparkServingIntent {
  SparkServingIntent {
    lifecycle: SurvivesReboot,
    availability: RestartsOnFailure,
    authority: ManagedConvergenceOwned,
    service_identity: ExecutorOwnedIdentity,
    endpoint: spark_serving_desired_endpoint_intent,
    observability: IndependentlyObservable,
  }
}

fn spark_serving_automation_principal_login() -> NonEmptyStr {
  "gunbc-automation" as NonEmptyStr
}

fn spark_serving_automation_principal() -> NamedPrincipal {
  NamedPrincipal { login: spark_serving_automation_principal_login() }
}


// THE FLEET-GLOBAL CONFIGURATION DROP IS RETIRED. Its restoration trigger read: no realization can
// be served at a configuration it has not been observed at, AND the fleet-global default no longer
// confers automatic admission. Both halves now hold by construction in
// gunbc.spark.serving_realization_config -- the config is looked up per (release, engine) key with
// no default arm -- so the two rows the drop named are DELETED here rather than left to feed a
// profile nothing should read. Per DESIGN section 4b(4) the evidence stays: the witness that the
// live realization refuses today is the executing proof the wall is real.
